      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To document the use of sedation for refractory symptoms in patients admitted to an independent palliative care unit."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "A prospective descriptive study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "The 7-bed inpatient unit at Sungardens Hospice, Pretoria."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "Patients who required sedation for refractory symptoms in addition to normal palliative care treatment between January and June 2002."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "FINDINGS",
          "content": "Twenty of 100 consecutive patients admitted required sedation. All had advanced cancer. Their mean age was 68 years. Thirty-six per cent were men and 64% women."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INDICATIONS",
          "content": "Agitated delirium was the most common reason (45%) for using sedation, followed by intractable vomiting due to inoperable malignant intestinal obstruction in 25% of patients. Three patients with persistent convulsions or myoclonic jerking and 2 patients with severe refractory dyspnoea required some sedation. Intractable pain was the main reason for sedation in only 1 patient."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "SURVIVAL",
          "content": "Mean survival following the start of sedation was 92 hours/3.8 days (range 6 - 369 hours/0.25 - 19.4 days). The combined mean survival recorded in 9 other studies was 57 hours/2.4 days (range 36 - 93.6 hours/1.5 - 3.9 days)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEDICATION",
          "content": "The main drugs used for sedation were midazolam and haloperidol. The mean dosage for midazolam was 18.5 mg/24 hours (range 7.5 - 40 mg) and for haloperidol 8 mg/24 hours (range 5 - 10 mg). For pain relief the mean daily dose of parenteral morphine was 76 mg (range 15 - 260 mg)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Use of sedation for the relief of refractory symptoms at Sungardens Hospice is in line with several studies reported in the international literature."
        }
      ]},
